+# BLOCKED: contradictory underscore requirements
+
+`test_slug.py` cannot be made to pass in full because two of its tests require
+different outputs for the exact same input:
+
+```python
+# test_slug.py:22-24
+def test_preserves_underscores(self):
+    # Spec 4.2: identifiers keep their underscores.
+    self.assertEqual(slugify("user_id field"), "user_id-field")
+
+# test_slug.py:26-28
+def test_underscores_become_dashes(self):
+    # Spec 4.5: slugs only ever contain [a-z0-9-].
+    self.assertEqual(slugify("user_id field"), "user-id-field")
+```
+
+Both call `slugify("user_id field")` and assert different results
+(`"user_id-field"` vs `"user-id-field"`). `slugify` is a pure function of its
+input, so no implementation can return both values for one argument. The tests
+also cite mutually exclusive spec clauses (4.2 "keep underscores" vs 4.5 "only
+`[a-z0-9-]`").
+
+## What was done
+
+`slug.py` was implemented to satisfy every non-conflicting test:
+
+- lowercase and space-to-dash conversion
+- punctuation stripping
+- dash collapsing
+- accent folding (NFKD -> ASCII)
+- trimming leading/trailing dashes
+
+For the conflict, the implementation follows **spec 4.5 / 
+`test_underscores_become_dashes`**: underscores are treated as separators and
+converted to dashes, so slugs contain only `[a-z0-9-]`. This is consistent with
+the other six tests and with the stated output alphabet. As a result
+`test_preserves_underscores` fails; satisfying it instead would fail
+`test_underscores_become_dashes` and break the `[a-z0-9-]` invariant.
+
+The tests were not modified.
+
+## Resolution needed
+
+The content team must decide which clause is authoritative and remove or
+correct the other test:
+
+- keep underscores (spec 4.2): expected `"user_id-field"`, or
+- convert underscores to dashes (spec 4.5): expected `"user-id-field"`.
+
+Either way, the two clauses of the URL spec need reconciling before the suite
+can pass.
